Long-Term Storage with Thanos
Learning Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Understand the architecture and benefits of Thanos for long-term metrics storage
Install and configure Thanos components on a Linux system
Set up Thanos Sidecar with Prometheus for high availability
Configure object storage for long-term metrics retention
Implement Thanos Query for unified metric querying across multiple Prometheus instances
Set up Thanos Store Gateway for historical data access
Configure Thanos Compactor for data optimization
Verify the complete Thanos setup and query historical metrics
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Prometheus and monitoring concepts
Familiarity with Linux command line operations
Knowledge of YAML configuration files
Understanding of containerization concepts (Docker)
Basic networking knowledge (ports, services)
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated environment. The provided Linux machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will install all required components during the lab exercises.

Task 1: Install Thanos and Configure it with Prometheus
Subtask 1.1: System Preparation and Dependencies Installation
First, let's prepare the system and install necessary dependencies.

# Update the system
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install required packages
sudo apt install -y wget curl unzip docker.io docker-compose

# Start and enable Docker
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker

# Add current user to docker group
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

# Apply group changes (you may need to log out and back in)
newgrp docker

# Create working directories
mkdir -p ~/thanos-lab/{prometheus,thanos,storage}
cd ~/thanos-lab
Subtask 1.2: Install Prometheus
Download and install Prometheus first, as Thanos works alongside it.

# Download Prometheus
cd ~/thanos-lab
wget https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases/download/v2.47.0/prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract Prometheus
tar -xzf prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz
mv prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64 prometheus-install

# Copy binaries to system path
sudo cp prometheus-install/prometheus /usr/local/bin/
sudo cp prometheus-install/promtool /usr/local/bin/

# Create prometheus user and directories
sudo useradd --no-create-home --shell /bin/false prometheus
sudo mkdir -p /etc/prometheus /var/lib/prometheus
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus /var/lib/prometheus
Subtask 1.3: Configure Prometheus for Thanos Integration
Create a Prometheus configuration that's compatible with Thanos.

# Create Prometheus configuration
sudo tee /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml > /dev/null <<EOF
global:
  scrape_interval: 15s
  evaluation_interval: 15s
  external_labels:
    cluster: 'thanos-lab'
    replica: 'prometheus-1'

rule_files:
  # - "first_rules.yml"
  # - "second_rules.yml"

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'prometheus'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9090']
  
  - job_name: 'node-exporter'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9100']
  
  - job_name: 'thanos-sidecar'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:10902']
  
  - job_name: 'thanos-query'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:10904']
EOF

# Set proper ownership
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Subtask 1.4: Install Node Exporter for Metrics
Install Node Exporter to generate some metrics for testing.

# Download Node Exporter
cd ~/thanos-lab
wget https://github.com/prometheus/node_exporter/releases/download/v1.6.1/node_exporter-1.6.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract and install
tar -xzf node_exporter-1.6.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz
sudo cp node_exporter-1.6.1.linux-amd64/node_exporter /usr/local/bin/

# Create systemd service for Node Exporter
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/node_exporter.service > /dev/null <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=Node Exporter
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/node_exporter

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

# Start Node Exporter
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start node_exporter
sudo systemctl enable node_exporter
Subtask 1.5: Download and Install Thanos
Now let's download and install Thanos components.

# Download Thanos
cd ~/thanos-lab
wget https://github.com/thanos-io/thanos/releases/download/v0.32.4/thanos-0.32.4.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract Thanos
tar -xzf thanos-0.32.4.linux-amd64.tar.gz
sudo cp thanos-0.32.4.linux-amd64/thanos /usr/local/bin/

# Verify installation
thanos --version
Subtask 1.6: Set Up MinIO for Object Storage
Thanos requires object storage. We'll use MinIO as a local S3-compatible storage.

# Download and install MinIO
cd ~/thanos-lab
wget https://dl.min.io/server/minio/release/linux-amd64/minio
chmod +x minio
sudo mv minio /usr/local/bin/

# Create MinIO directories
mkdir -p ~/thanos-lab/storage/minio-data

# Create MinIO configuration
mkdir -p ~/.minio

# Start MinIO server
cd ~/thanos-lab/storage
nohup minio server minio-data --address :9000 --console-address :9001 > minio.log 2>&1 &

# Wait for MinIO to start
sleep 10

# Check if MinIO is running
curl -I http://localhost:9000/minio/health/live
Subtask 1.7: Configure MinIO and Create Bucket
Set up MinIO client and create a bucket for Thanos.

# Download MinIO client
cd ~/thanos-lab
wget https://dl.min.io/client/mc/release/linux-amd64/mc
chmod +x mc
sudo mv mc /usr/local/bin/

# Configure MinIO client (default credentials: minioadmin/minioadmin)
mc alias set local http://localhost:9000 minioadmin minioadmin

# Create bucket for Thanos
mc mb local/thanos-bucket

# Verify bucket creation
mc ls local/
Subtask 1.8: Create Thanos Object Storage Configuration
Create the configuration file for Thanos to use MinIO.

# Create Thanos bucket configuration
mkdir -p ~/thanos-lab/thanos/config

tee ~/thanos-lab/thanos/config/bucket.yml > /dev/null <<EOF
type: S3
config:
  bucket: "thanos-bucket"
  endpoint: "localhost:9000"
  access_key: "minioadmin"
  secret_key: "minioadmin"
  insecure: true
  signature_version2: false
  http_config:
    idle_conn_timeout: 90s
    response_header_timeout: 2m
    insecure_skip_verify: true
EOF
Task 2: Set Up High-Availability Prometheus with Thanos
Subtask 2.1: Create Prometheus Systemd Service with Thanos Integration
Create a systemd service for Prometheus that enables external labels and remote write.

# Create Prometheus systemd service
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/prometheus.service > /dev/null <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=Prometheus
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/prometheus \\
    --config.file=/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml \\
    --storage.tsdb.path=/var/lib/prometheus/ \\
    --web.console.templates=/etc/prometheus/consoles \\
    --web.console.libraries=/etc/prometheus/console_libraries \\
    --web.listen-address=0.0.0.0:9090 \\
    --web.enable-lifecycle \\
    --storage.tsdb.min-block-duration=2h \\
    --storage.tsdb.max-block-duration=2h \\
    --storage.tsdb.retention.time=6h

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

# Start Prometheus
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start prometheus
sudo systemctl enable prometheus

# Check Prometheus status
sudo systemctl status prometheus
Subtask 2.2: Configure and Start Thanos Sidecar
The Thanos Sidecar runs alongside Prometheus and uploads data to object storage.

# Create Thanos Sidecar systemd service
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/thanos-sidecar.service > /dev/null <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=Thanos Sidecar
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/thanos sidecar \\
    --tsdb.path=/var/lib/prometheus \\
    --prometheus.url=http://localhost:9090 \\
    --grpc-address=0.0.0.0:10901 \\
    --http-address=0.0.0.0:10902 \\
    --objstore.config-file=/home/$USER/thanos-lab/thanos/config/bucket.yml

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

# Start Thanos Sidecar
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start thanos-sidecar
sudo systemctl enable thanos-sidecar

# Check Thanos Sidecar status
sudo systemctl status thanos-sidecar
Subtask 2.3: Set Up Thanos Query
Thanos Query provides a unified query interface across multiple Prometheus instances.

# Create Thanos Query systemd service
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/thanos-query.service > /dev/null <<EOF
